For a while I had been having problems finding where things live in the c10 dispatcher. So I decided to sit down and trace out the data composition diagram to understand the data model of the dispatcher. Here's what I got, when reading the state of the tree after #23668.
// KernelFunction.h

using KernelFunction = void(Stack*, KernelCache* cache);

// DispatchTable.h

struct DispatchTableEntry {
  KernelFunction* kernel_func;  // can be nullptr, not all kernels have this
  /*not-nullable*/ KernelCacheCreatorFunction cache_creator_func;
  void* unboxed_kernel_func; // can be nullptr, not all kernels have this
};

class impl::KernelTable {
 ska::flat_hash_map<TensorTypeId, DispatchTableEntry> map_;
}

class DispatchTable {
  detail::KernelTable_ kernels_;
  c10::optional<DispatchTableEntry> catchall_kernel_;
  DispatchStrategy dispatch_strategy_;
  std::string operator_name_;
}

// OperatorEntry.h

class OpKernel {
  KernelFunction* kernel_;
  std::unique_ptr<c10::KernelCache> cache_;
  void* unboxed_kernel_;
}

class impl::OperatorEntry {
  FunctionSchema schema_;
  LeftRight<DispatchTable> dispatchTable_;
  c10::either<
    ska::flat_hash_map<TensorTypeId, std::list<DispatchTableEntry>>, // dispatched kernels
    std::list<DispatchTableEntry> // catch-all kernels
  > kernels_;
  std::list<void*> unboxedAutogradKernels_; // ???
  std::atomic<void*> currentUnboxedAutogradKernel_; // ???
  OperatorOptions options_;
  std::mutex kernelsMutex_; // protects kernels_
  std::mutex unboxedAutogradKernelsMutex_; // protects unboxedAutogradKernels_
}

// operator_name.h

struct OperatorName final {
  std::string name;
  std::string overload_name;
};

// Dispatcher.h

// Morally this is just map<OperatorName, OperatorEntry>

struct OperatorDef {
  impl::OperatorEntry op;
  size_t refcount;
};

class OperatorHandle {
  std::list<Dispatcher::OperatorDef>::iterator operatorIterator_;
}

class Dispatcher {
  std::list<OperatorDef> operators_;
  LeftRight<ska::flat_hash_map<OperatorName, OperatorHandle>> operatorLookupTable_;
  std::unique_ptr<detail::RegistrationListenerList> listeners_;
  std::mutex mutex_;
}

I have a number of questions:

The naming is terrible. What does a "KernelTable" contain? DispatchTableEntries, of course!
There are two kernel tables, of some sort: there is a kernels_ member in OperatorEntry, and another kernels_ in DispatchTable. This is pretty weird; why do we need the field in two places?
Do we still need registration listeners? I never understood their motivation
Do we really need to be able to register an OperatorDef to multiple OperatorNames? I can't think of a legitimate use case for this functionality.
Should OperatorName really be a pair of std::string? What about a schema string?
After VariableTensorId was added, we can get rid of unboxedAutogradKernels_ and friends, right?
Why are there multiple mutexes in OperatorEntry?
I've previously argued that we shouldn't call the kernel cache a "cache"; it's the data associated with the closure. Following on with that, there's no reason for kernel_cache to be a unique_ptr if we're just going to use it to store function pointers, right?

