For benchmarking purposes, sometimes we make little native functions which don't do any tensor allocations:
Tensor _const5(const Tensor& self, const Tensor& second, const Tensor& third, const Tensor& fourth, const Tensor& fifth) {  return self;
}

This results in the following Variable code being generated:
  auto tmp = ([&]() {
    at::AutoNonVariableTypeMode non_var_type_mode(true);
    return at::_const5(self_, second_, third_, fourth_, fifth_);
  })();
  auto result = as_variable(std::move(tmp));
  if (grad_fn) {
      set_history(flatten_tensor_args( result ), grad_fn);
  }

The hazard is that if grad_fn is the same Tensor as self, we will mutate the input argument (bad bad).
User-defined autograd functions have an explicit check for when output aliases with input, but for native functions we just assume that people have written them correctly. This might not be a good assumption.
